       dc(1) is an arbitrary-precision calculator.  It uses a stack (reverse
       Polish notation) to store numbers and results of computations.
       Arithmetic operations pop arguments off of the stack and push the
       results.

       If no files are given on the command-line, then dc(1) reads from ssttddiinn
       (see the SSTTDDIINN section).  Otherwise, those files are processed, and
       dc(1) will then exit.

       If a user wants to set up a standard environment, they can use
       DDCC__EENNVV__AARRGGSS (see the EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT VVAARRIIAABBLLEESS section).  For example, if a
       user wants the ssccaallee always set to 1100, they can set DDCC__EENNVV__AARRGGSS to --ee
       1100kk, and this dc(1) will always start with a ssccaallee of 1100.

