       Most dc(1) implementations use cchhaarr types to calculate the value of 11
       decimal digit at a time, but that can be slow.  This dc(1) does
       something different.

       It uses large integers to calculate more than 11 decimal digit at a
       time.  If built in a environment where DDCC__LLOONNGG__BBIITT (see the LLIIMMIITTSS
       section) is 6644, then each integer has 99 decimal digits.  If built in an
       environment where DDCC__LLOONNGG__BBIITT is 3322 then each integer has 44 decimal
       digits.  This value (the number of decimal digits per large integer) is
       called DDCC__BBAASSEE__DDIIGGSS.

       In addition, this dc(1) uses an even larger integer for overflow
       checking.  This integer type depends on the value of DDCC__LLOONNGG__BBIITT, but
       is always at least twice as large as the integer type used to store
       digits.

